  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nWave of interest for this evening shows up nicely on water vapor \nsatellite imagery over eastern MT. This wave will continue moving \neastward into the nighttime hours while a surface low also tracks \nsoutheast across the area. Latest models continue to show light rain \npotential across the northern third of SD into west central MN \nthrough the late afternoon and evening hours. PoPs have been \nincreased slightly across the northern CWA, generally from 20 to 40 \npercent. It still appears any rainfall amounts will be below \n0.25in, and likely even less than 0.10in.\n\nMid/upper flow then becomes northerly Wed-Fri, while a surface ridge \naxis sets up shop and looks to stay in place for some time. By \nSaturday morning, the ridge axis has only moved into MN, while still \nlargely having an influence on our weather. Colder air aloft still \nset to spill southward across the region, more so the eastern CWA \nwhere 850mb temps drop below zero C. High temps Thursday (and now \nFriday) are only in the upper 30s for the eastern CWA. What has been \nnoticed in the models is that the colder air lingers around longer \nover the area before the next warm up begins. We really don't see \nwidespread 50s returning to the area now until Monday and Tuesday, \nwhereas 24 to 48 hours ago it appeared we would start warming up by \nSunday already.",
